= Part (b)

Even-length strings whose second half contains an even number of 1s, that is,

$
{x y in Sigma^* | |x| = |y| "and" \#(1, y) "is even"}.
$

#boxed[
$
X &-> 0 | 1 & #h(1.5em) "the two one-symbol strings" \
E &-> epsilon | X E 0 | X O 1 & #h(1.5em) "even-length, second half has an even number of 1s" \
O &-> X O 0 | X E 1 & #h(1.5em) "even-length, second half has an odd number of 1s"
$
]

The start symbol is $E$. The three non-terminals generate the following
languages.

- $X$ generates the two strings $0$ and $1$.

- $E$ generates every string $x y$ with $|x| = |y|$ and $\#(1, y)$ even.

- $O$ generates every string $x y$ with $|x| = |y|$ and $\#(1, y)$ odd.

*Why this is correct.* Every rule other than $E -> epsilon$ adds one symbol at
the far left and one symbol at the far right, so a derivation of a string of
length $2 n$ applies exactly $n$ of them. The $n$ symbols added on the left, in
the order they were added, spell out the first half $x$, and the $n$ symbols
added on the right spell out the second half $y$ from its last symbol backwards.
The halves therefore always have equal length, and the only thing the rules need
to track is the parity of $\#(1, y)$.

- A symbol 0 placed on the right keeps that parity, which is why $E -> X E 0$ and
  $O -> X O 0$ leave the non-terminal unchanged.

- A symbol 1 placed on the right flips it, which is why $E -> X O 1$ and
  $O -> X E 1$ switch between the two non-terminals.

- The base case $E -> epsilon$ is correct because the empty second half contains
  zero 1s, and $O$ has no $epsilon$-rule for the same reason.

Formally, both inclusions follow by induction on $n$. Each rule preserves the
claimed description, as the previous paragraph shows. For the other direction,
let $x y$ have $|x| = |y| = n$.

- If $n = 0$, then $\#(1, y) = 0$ is even and $E -> epsilon$ applies.

- If $n >= 1$, write $x = a x'$ and $y = y' b$ with $a, b in Sigma$. Then
  $|x'| = |y'| = n - 1$, and $\#(1, y) = \#(1, y') + 1$ when $b = 1$, while
  $\#(1, y) = \#(1, y')$ when $b = 0$. So if $\#(1, y)$ is even, then $x' y'$ is
  generated by $E$ when $b = 0$ and by $O$ when $b = 1$, and the rules
  $E -> X E 0$ and $E -> X O 1$ produce $x y$. The odd case is symmetric.

#disclosures(llm: llm-note)

