= Part (c)

$
{0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d | a, b, c, d >= 1 "and if" a = d "then" b = c}
$

#boxed[
$
S &-> O M I | G | H & #h(1.5em) "the language" \
G &-> 0 G 1 | 0 O I O 1 & #h(1.5em) 0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d "with" a > d \
H &-> 0 H 1 | 0 I O I 1 & #h(1.5em) 0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d "with" a < d \
M &-> 1 0 | 1 M 0 & #h(1.5em) 1^n 0^n "with" n >= 1 \
O &-> 0 | 0 O & #h(1.5em) 0^n "with" n >= 1 \
I &-> 1 | 1 I & #h(1.5em) 1^n "with" n >= 1
$
]

The six non-terminals generate the following languages.

- $O$ generates every non-empty string of 0s, that is, every $0^n$ with $n >= 1$.

- $I$ generates every non-empty string of 1s, that is, every $1^n$ with $n >= 1$.

- $M$ generates every string $1^n 0^n$ with $n >= 1$.

- $G$ generates every string $0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d$ with $a, b, c, d >= 1$ and $a > d$.

- $H$ generates every string $0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d$ with $a, b, c, d >= 1$ and $a < d$.

- $S$ generates every string $0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d$ with $a, b, c, d >= 1$ such that
  $b = c$ whenever $a = d$, which is the language asked for.

*Why this is correct.* The condition "if $a = d$ then $b = c$" is the same as
"$a != d$ or $b = c$", so the language is the union of three sets: the strings
with $b = c$, the strings with $a > d$, and the strings with $a < d$. The three
alternatives for $S$ generate exactly those three sets.

- $O M I$ generates $0^a 1^n 0^n 1^d$ for all $a, n, d >= 1$, which is exactly
  the set of strings of the required shape with $b = c$.

- A derivation from $G$ applies $G -> 0 G 1$ some $k >= 0$ times and then
  $G -> 0 O I O 1$, producing

  $
  0^k (0 med 0^p 1^q 0^r 1) 1^k = 0^(k + 1 + p) 1^q 0^r 1^(k + 1)
  $

  for some $p, q, r >= 1$. So $a = k + 1 + p$, $b = q$, $c = r$, $d = k + 1$, and
  $a - d = p >= 1$. Conversely, any string $0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d$ with $b, c >= 1$ and
  $a > d >= 1$ arises this way, by taking $k = d - 1$, $p = a - d$, $q = b$, and
  $r = c$.

- A derivation from $H$ likewise produces

  $
  0^k (0 med 1^p 0^q 1^r 1) 1^k = 0^(k + 1) 1^p 0^q 1^(r + 1 + k)
  $

  for some $p, q, r >= 1$. So $a = k + 1$, $b = p$, $c = q$, $d = k + 1 + r$, and
  $d - a = r >= 1$. Conversely, any string with $b, c >= 1$ and $d > a >= 1$
  arises by taking $k = a - 1$, $p = b$, $q = c$, and $r = d - a$.

Every string generated by $S$ therefore has the shape $0^a 1^b 0^c 1^d$ with all
four exponents at least 1, and satisfies $a != d$ or $b = c$, so it is in the
language. Conversely, a string of the language with $a = d$ must have $b = c$ and
is generated by $O M I$, and a string with $a != d$ is generated by $G$ or by
$H$. The grammar is ambiguous, since strings with $b = c$ and $a != d$ have
derivations of both kinds, but that is permitted here.

